WEBVTT

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.240
₹315.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.536
That is a day's wage for a woman doing
casual work in India.

00:00:11.616 --> 00:00:12.990
₹455 for a man.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:18.330
One wasted trip to a government office
costs her that whole day.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.588
India has about 44 crore unorganised
workers.

00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:27.240
31 crore are already registered on
e-Shram.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.315
The schemes exist.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:33.060
What nobody tells her is whether one will
accept her.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.290
So I built something that answers before
she goes.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:39.897
I'm Avinash.

00:00:39.977 --> 00:00:41.880
This is Yojana Sathi.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.682
A short chat, in Hindi or English, that
tells a worker which schemes fit,

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:49.980
what to carry, and where to go.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.553
In the next few minutes I'll show you how
it is built.

00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:56.910
Every tool, and why it is there.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.810
It began as a hackathon.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.120
My first project was Saans, an air-
exposure assistant.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.639
I built it to completion.

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:12.650
Then I noticed nobody had asked for it.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.658
So I parked it.

00:01:14.738 --> 00:01:21.250
The lesson stayed: real users beat a
repository that only looks finished.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.713
I'll be direct about this.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:27.110
Yojana Sathi was built with AI.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:32.848
Claude Code wrote most of the code, the
tests and the scripts.

00:01:32.928 --> 00:01:36.040
ChatGPT and Codex gave second opinions.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.613
What I owned was everything else.

00:01:39.693 --> 00:01:40.759
The decisions.

00:01:40.839 --> 00:01:41.742
The sources.

00:01:41.822 --> 00:01:42.807
The accounts.

00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:43.708
The server.

00:01:43.788 --> 00:01:46.900
And the call on what was safe to ship.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.520
Before writing a line, I made four
decisions.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.760
The AI never decides eligibility.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.710
There are three answers, not two.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.600
A missing fact is written as TODO, never
as zero.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.396
And no third-party packages.

00:02:05.476 --> 00:02:07.730
Python's standard library only.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.300
Everything you'll see follows from those
four.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.850
Here is the whole system.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.289
Her phone.

00:02:18.369 --> 00:02:22.590
Three doors: Telegram, WhatsApp, and a web
page.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.783
One server in Mumbai.

00:02:24.863 --> 00:02:27.540
One rule engine behind every door.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.360
The project has zero dependencies.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.793
On Saans, I had planned three popular
libraries.

00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:43.540
Each turned out to be about 80 lines of
Python's own urllib.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.632
So here I started from the standard
library.

00:02:47.712 --> 00:02:52.746
HTTP, the database, the rule files, the
web server, the cryptography.

00:02:52.826 --> 00:02:53.790
All built in.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.140
There is no install step that can fail on
deploy day.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.600
Each scheme's rules live in a TOML file.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.825
Python reads TOML with no library.

00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:09.891
And a comment can sit right beside a
value,

00:03:09.971 --> 00:03:14.250
so every threshold carries the official
source it came from.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.960
A reviewer at an NGO can check a rule
without reading any code.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.590
Rules written in Python would be
unreviewable to the people who matter
most.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.438
Everything I count lands in SQLite.

00:03:30.518 --> 00:03:31.293
One file.

00:03:31.373 --> 00:03:33.180
No database server.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.193
One module is its only writer.

00:03:36.273 --> 00:03:37.890
One place to audit.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.716
And it holds coarse facts only.

00:03:41.796 --> 00:03:47.748
There is no name column, no phone column,
no Aadhaar column.

00:03:47.828 --> 00:03:52.150
A field that does not exist can never
leak.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.491
One function decides eligibility.

00:03:56.571 --> 00:03:58.372
It is a pure function.

00:03:58.452 --> 00:04:01.530
No network, no clock, no randomness.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.029
Same answers in, same verdict out.

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:06.050
Every time.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:12.620
When she taps "don't know", the answer
stays unknown, all the way to her screen.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:20.070
And a test fails the build if the rules
code ever imports a model or an HTTP
client.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:27.872
Claude has one optional job: turning a job
typed in her own words into a category

00:04:27.952 --> 00:04:28.960
she confirms.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.610
It never sees a rule, a rupee figure, or a
verdict.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.376
Running without it is a tested setting.

00:04:37.456 --> 00:04:40.479
On the server today, it is switched off.

00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:43.430
The answers are identical either way.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.554
The server runs on AWS.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:56.220
One EC2 t4g.micro, an ARM machine, in
Mumbai, the region closest to her.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.809
I built it with the AWS CLI, from one
script.

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:00.575
No clicking.

00:05:00.655 --> 00:05:01.930
The same every time.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.756
It checks which account it is on.

00:05:05.836 --> 00:05:09.564
It keeps the private key on my laptop.

00:05:09.644 --> 00:05:12.850
It opens SSH to my address only.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.260
And it sets a zero-spend budget alert, so
I hear the moment real money starts.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.398
Inside, three services run from one
codebase.

00:05:26.478 --> 00:05:29.050
Telegram, WhatsApp, and the web.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:34.560
systemd keeps each one alive and restarts
any that falls.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:41.510
Each runs as its own user, with no login
shell, and can write to one folder only.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.296
A timer copies the database every night at
3 a.m., Indian time,

00:05:47.376 --> 00:05:50.060
when the fewest people are using it.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.362
The bot token, the WhatsApp token, the app
secret.

00:05:55.442 --> 00:06:00.510
They live in one locked file on the server
that only root can read.

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.750
They never go to GitHub.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.668
Deploying merges my laptop's settings into
that file.

00:06:07.748 --> 00:06:09.272
It never replaces it.

00:06:09.352 --> 00:06:15.310
An earlier version did, and quietly
dropped settings that only the server had.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.430
Deploying is a second script, over SSH.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.510
It copies the code, never my laptop's
database.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.428
Then the gate.

00:06:26.508 --> 00:06:29.590
Every test runs on the server first.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.525
If one fails, the live code is untouched.

00:06:33.605 --> 00:06:37.310
If all pass, every service restarts
together.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:47.970
Telegram was first, because it was the
easiest door to build and test.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:55.390
My server asks Telegram for messages and
waits up to 50 seconds for an answer.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.107
That is long polling.

00:06:57.187 --> 00:07:03.341
A webhook would need a public address, a
certificate, and a host that never sleeps.

00:07:03.421 --> 00:07:06.500
Three things that can fail on deploy day.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.730
Long polling needs one outgoing
connection.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.838
WhatsApp works the other way round.

00:07:16.918 --> 00:07:18.470
Meta calls my server.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.404
Every message carries a signature, sealed
with my app secret.

00:07:23.484 --> 00:07:26.400
My server checks it before reading a word.

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.380
Without the secret, the service refuses to
start.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:35.588
WhatsApp is tested end to end on Meta's
test number.

00:07:35.668 --> 00:07:37.275
It is not public yet.

00:07:37.355 --> 00:07:40.890
Code that works is not the same as a
launch.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:46.586
For everyone without either app, there is
a web page.

00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:47.453
No account.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:48.400
No install.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.158
Because it is public, it has guards.

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.890
Small requests only.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.622
A 10-second timeout.

00:07:55.702 --> 00:07:59.087
Sessions that end after 30 idle minutes.

00:07:59.167 --> 00:08:03.930
And a cap on new sessions, so no one can
crowd her out.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:07.790
In front of everything sits Caddy.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:09.987
One door, port 443.

00:08:10.067 --> 00:08:13.308
It sends each request to the right
service,

00:08:13.388 --> 00:08:16.940
and gets free certificates from Let's
Encrypt.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.410
My domain's DNS points two names at that
one door.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.630
And Caddy is where I found a privacy leak.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:31.088
My Python code never logged a worker's
private sheet link.

00:08:31.168 --> 00:08:36.130
But Caddy, in front of it, logged every
one, with her IP address.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:42.300
Now Caddy deletes the address and blanks
the link before it writes a single line.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:47.360
Privacy belongs to the whole system, not
to one function.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.360
Every change goes through GitHub.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.759
GitHub Actions runs the full check on
every push.

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:06.130
Then it builds a Docker image, and the
image runs every check again while it
builds.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.290
An image that fails its own tests cannot
exist.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.880
A second workflow publishes the website to
GitHub Pages.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.972
There is no test framework.

00:09:20.052 --> 00:09:26.190
Plain asserts, a self-check in every
module, and one command that runs them
all.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:33.910
One walker presses every button on every
screen, in both languages.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:35.000
954 paths.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:41.076
Another rewrites each scheme's rules from
the official text, separately,

00:09:41.156 --> 00:09:45.490
and compares the two across more than 3
million combinations.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.289
They catch different failures.

00:09:48.369 --> 00:09:51.800
Pressing buttons can never catch a wrong
threshold.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.687
The day it went live, I used it like a
worker.

00:10:01.767 --> 00:10:04.140
It broke within the hour.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:05.731
Six bugs.

00:10:05.811 --> 00:10:08.318
Telegram rejected a message.

00:10:08.398 --> 00:10:12.105
Hinglish like "mistri" went unrecognised.

00:10:12.185 --> 00:10:15.510
An English screen had Hindi buttons.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.592
Every one was in the conversation.

00:10:18.672 --> 00:10:22.090
None was in the rule engine I had tested
hardest.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:26.780
The most serious bug hid in a double tap.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.581
A button said only "yes", not which
question it answered.

00:10:32.661 --> 00:10:35.600
On a slow network, she taps again.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.320
By then, the next question is income tax.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.644
Some pensions exclude taxpayers.

00:10:42.724 --> 00:10:46.250
One impatient tap could change her
verdict.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:50.519
Now a tap on an old button is refused.

00:10:50.599 --> 00:10:54.108
On my laptop, a double tap looked silly.

00:10:54.188 --> 00:10:56.610
On her phone, it is normal.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:05.130
The worst bug was mine, and ChatGPT found
it in an outside review.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.504
My code treated a finished file as a
verified one.

00:11:09.584 --> 00:11:14.660
Researched data was serving real verdicts
before anyone had checked it.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:19.873
Now only a scheme signed by a person can
answer.

00:11:19.953 --> 00:11:21.616
The demo got worse.

00:11:21.696 --> 00:11:22.980
It got honest.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:29.345
My impact number was adding a yearly
pension to an insurance cover.

00:11:29.425 --> 00:11:30.863
It looked impressive.

00:11:30.943 --> 00:11:32.100
It was nonsense.

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.207
They are two separate numbers now.

00:11:35.287 --> 00:11:40.452
And when I checked my own headline
statistics, three of four were wrong.

00:11:40.532 --> 00:11:42.273
One had no source at all.

00:11:42.353 --> 00:11:43.300
I deleted it.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.580
The server found bugs my laptop couldn't.

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:51.368
The logs looked empty while the bot was
healthy.

00:11:51.448 --> 00:11:53.380
Python was holding them back.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:59.930
And my update script restarted stopped
services, but left running ones on old
code.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:07.110
Today it is live on Telegram and on the
web.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:12.740
21 schemes, each signed by a person, serve
real verdicts.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:18.832
Two drafts, Tamil Nadu disability and
Mizoram old-age, still answer unknown,

00:12:18.912 --> 00:12:23.420
because I could not find an official list
of the papers they need.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:26.432
There is no field pilot yet.

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:29.715
No worker outside my own testing has used
it.

00:12:29.795 --> 00:12:33.150
That matters more than any of the
engineering.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:36.982
Writing code became cheap.

00:12:37.062 --> 00:12:38.840
Trusting it did not.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.325
A green test can lie.

00:12:41.406 --> 00:12:44.480
A running process is not a healthy one.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:48.632
A finished file is not a verified one.

00:12:48.712 --> 00:12:51.630
A working adapter is not a launch.

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:56.910
The behaviour I value most looks the least
impressive.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.506
The bot saying: I don't know.

00:12:59.586 --> 00:13:01.910
Ask this question at the office.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.150
She does not need a confident bot.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.680
She needs the right trip, or none.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:10.382
Yojana Sathi.

00:13:10.462 --> 00:13:12.190
Know before you go.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:14.883
The code is public on GitHub.

00:13:14.963 --> 00:13:16.049
The bot is live.

00:13:16.129 --> 00:13:20.210
And I'm looking for my first role as a
Python developer.
